    "behaviour": "shared",
    "chain": "Okay, so I need to express the statement \"There exists exactly one number that is both prime and even\" in predicate logic using P(x) for \"x is prime\" and E(x) for \"x is even. Then, I have to determine if this statement is true or false in the domain of positive integers.\n\nFirst, let me break down the statement. It says there's exactly one number that is both prime and even. So, in logical terms, that would be: there exists an x such that x is prime and x is even, and for all y, if y is prime and even, then y equals x. That makes sense because \"exactly one\" means uniqueness.\n\nSo, translating that into predicate logic, it should be:\n\n∃x (P(x) ∧ E(x)) ∧ ∀y (P(y) ∧ E(y) → y = x)\n\nLet me write that out step by step. The first part is the existence of such an x, which is ∃x (P(x) ∧ E(x)). The second part is that this x is unique, so for all y, if y is prime and even, then y must be equal to x. So, that's ∀y (P(y) ∧ E(y) → y = x). So, putting it together, it's an existential quantifier followed by a universal quantifier.\n\nNow, moving on to the second part, which is to prove whether this statement is true or false in the domain of positive integers. Hmm, okay. Let's recall what prime numbers and even numbers are.\n\nA prime number is a natural number greater than 1 that has no positive divisors other than 1 and itself. The smallest prime number is 2. An even number is any integer that is divisible by 2, so numbers like 2, 4, 6, 8, etc.\n\nSo, the only even prime number is 2 because any other even number is divisible by 2, which would make it composite. For example, 4 is divisible by 2 and 4, so it's not prime. 6 is divisible by 2 and 3, so not prime, and so on. Therefore, 2 is the only even prime number.\n\nSo, if we're looking for a number that is both prime and even, it must be 2. Therefore, there exists exactly one such number, which is 2. So, the statement should be true.\n\nWait, but let me make sure I'm not missing anything. The domain is positive integers, so we're talking about numbers starting from 1, 2, 3, etc. But 1 is not considered a prime number because prime numbers are defined as having exactly two distinct positive divisors. 1 only has one divisor, which is itself, so it's not prime. Therefore, the only candidate is 2, which is prime and even. So, that's the only number that satisfies both conditions.\n\nTherefore, the statement is true because there exists exactly one number (which is 2) that is both prime and even in the domain of positive integers.\n\nBut just to be thorough, let me consider if there could be any other numbers that are both prime and even. Let's think about 3. 3 is prime, but it's odd. 5 is prime and odd. 7 is prime and odd. 11 is prime and odd. 13 is prime and odd. 17 is prime and odd. 19 is prime and odd. 23 is prime and odd. 29 is prime and odd. 31 is prime and odd. 37 is prime and odd. 41 is prime and odd. 43 is prime and odd. 47 is prime and odd. 53 is prime and odd. 59 is prime and odd. 61 is prime and odd. 67 is prime and odd. 71 is prime and odd. 73 is prime and odd. 79 is prime and odd. 83 is prime and odd. 89 is prime and odd. 97 is prime and odd. 101 is prime and odd. 103 is prime and odd. 107 is prime and odd. 109 is prime and odd. 113 is prime and odd. 127 is prime and odd. 131 is prime and odd. 137 is prime and odd. 139 is prime and odd. 149 is prime and odd. 151 is prime and odd. 157 is prime and odd. 163 is prime and odd. 167 is prime and odd. 173 is prime and odd. 179 is prime and odd. 181 is prime and odd. 191 is prime and odd. 193 is prime and odd. 197 is prime and odd. 199 is prime and odd. 211 is prime and odd. 223 is prime and odd. 227 is prime and odd. 229 is prime and odd. 233 is prime and odd. 239 is prime and odd. 241 is prime and odd. 251 is prime and odd. 257 is prime and odd. 263 is prime and odd. 269 is prime and odd. 271 is prime and odd. 277 is prime and odd. 281 is prime and odd. 283 is prime and odd. 293 is prime and odd. 307 is prime