      {
        "label": "deduction",
        "text": "So, in logical terms, that would be: there exists an x such that x is prime and x is even, and for all y, if y is prime and even, then y equals x. That makes sense because \"exactly one\" means uniqueness."
      },
      {
        "label": "deduction",
        "text": "So, translating that into predicate logic, it should be:\n\n∃x (P(x) ∧ E(x)) ∧ ∀y (P(y) ∧ E(y) → y = x)"
      },
      {
        "label": "deduction",
        "text": "Let me write that out step by step. The first part is the existence of such an x, which is ∃x (P(x) ∧ E(x)). The second part is that this x is unique, so for all y, if y is prime and even, then y must be equal to x. So, that's ∀y (P(y) ∧ E(y) → y = x). So, putting it together, it's an existential quantifier followed by a universal quantifier."
      },
