    "chain_full": "Okay, so I need to prove that (A ∪ B) - C is equal to (A - C) ∪ (B - C). Hmm, set operations can be a bit tricky, but I think I can handle this by using the definitions provided. Let me recall that for any set X and Y, x ∈ (X - Y) if and only if x is in X and x is not in Y. So, I need to show that every element in (A ∪ B) - C is also in (A - C) ∪ (B - C), and vice versa. That should establish the equality.\n\nFirst, let me write down what each side means using the definition. \n\nStarting with the left-hand side: (A ∪ B) - C. By definition, an element x is in (A ∪ B) - C if and only if x is in (A ∪ B) and x is not in C. \n\nNow, (A ∪ B) is the set of all elements that are in A or in B or in both. So, x ∈ (A ∪ B) means x is in A or x is in B. Therefore, (A ∪ B) - C is the set of all elements that are in A or B, but not in C.\n\nOn the other hand, the right-hand side is (A - C) ∪ (B - C). Let's unpack that. An element x is in (A - C) if x is in A and x is not in C. Similarly, x is in (B - C) if x is in B and x is not in C. So, the union of these two sets would include all elements that are in A but not in C, or in B but not in C, or in both.\n\nSo, to show that (A ∪ B) - C equals (A - C) ∪ (B - C), I need to show two things:\n\n1. Every element in (A ∪ B) - C is also in (A - C) ∪ (B - C).\n2. Every element in (A - C) ∪ (B - C) is also in (A ∪ B) - C.\n\nLet me tackle the first part first: proving that (A ∪ B) - C is a subset of (A - C) ∪ (B - C).\n\nTake an arbitrary element x. If x is in (A ∪ B) - C, then by definition, x is in A ∪ B and x is not in C. \n\nNow, since x is in A ∪ B, it must be in A or in B. So, there are two cases:\n\nCase 1: x is in A. Since x is not in C, x is in A - C. Therefore, x is in (A - C) ∪ (B - C).\n\nCase 2: x is in B. Similarly, since x is not in C, x is in B - C. Therefore, x is in (A - C) ∪ (B - C).\n\nIn both cases, x is in (A - C) ∪ (B - C). Therefore, every element of (A ∪ B) - C is also in (A - C) ∪ (B - C), which means (A ∪ B) - C is a subset of (A - C) ∪ (B - C).\n\nNow, for the second part: proving that (A - C) ∪ (B - C) is a subset of (A ∪ B) - C.\n\nTake an arbitrary element x. If x is in (A - C) ∪ (B - C), then x is in A - C or x is in B - C.\n\nCase 1: x is in A - C. Then, x is in A and x is not in C. Since x is in A, it is in A ∪ B, and since x is not in C, x is in (A ∪ B) - C.\n\nCase 2: x is in B - C. Then, x is in B and x is not in C. Similarly, x is in A ∪ B, and since x is not in C, x is in (A ∪ B) - C.\n\nIn both cases, x is in (A ∪ B) - C. Therefore, every element of (A - C) ∪ (B - C) is also in (A ∪ B) - C, which means (A - C) ∪ (B - C) is a subset of (A ∪ B) - C.\n\nSince we've shown both inclusions, we can conclude that (A ∪ B) - C equals (A - C) ∪ (B - C). \n\nWait, let me double-check if I missed anything. I considered all possible cases for x being in A or B, and in each case, I correctly concluded where x would be. Also, I made sure to use the definition of set difference correctly. Yeah, I think that's solid.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe equality is proven, so \\boxed{(A \\cup B) - C = (A - C) \\cup (B - C)}.\n</think>\n\nTo prove that \\((A \\cup B) - C = (A - C) \\cup (B - C)\\), we need to show mutual subset inclusion.\n\nFirst, we show that \\((A \\cup B) - C \\subseteq (A - C) \\cup (B - C)\\):\n\n1. Take an arbitrary element \\(x\\). If \\(x \\in (A \\cup B) - C\\), then \\(x \\in A \\cup B\\) and \\(x \\notin C\\).\n2. Since \\(x \\in A \\cup B\\), \\(x\\) is in \\(A\\) or \\(B\\).\n3. If \\(x \\in A\\), then \\(x \\in A - C\\), so \\(x \\in (A - C) \\cup (B - C)\\).\n4. If \\(x \\in B\\), then \\(x \\in B - C\\), so \\(x \\in (A - C) \\cup (B - C)\\).\n\nThus, every element of \\((A \\cup B) - C\\) is in \\((A - C) \\cup (B - C)\\), proving \\((A \\cup B) - C \\subseteq (A - C) \\cup (B - C)\\).\n\nNext, we show that \\((A - C) \\cup (B - C) \\subseteq (A \\cup B) - C\\):\n\n1. Take an arbitrary element \\(x\\). If \\(x \\in (A - C) \\cup (B - C)\\), then \\(x \\in A - C\\) or \\(x \\in B - C\\).\n2. If \\(x \\in A - C\\), then \\(x \\in A\\) and \\(x \\notin C\\), so \\(x \\in A \\cup B\\) and \\(x \\notin C\\), hence \\(x \\in (A \\cup B) - C\\).\n3. If \\(x \\in B - C\\), then \\(x \\in B\\) and \\(x \\notin C\\), so \\(x \\in A \\cup B\\) and \\(x \\notin C\\), hence \\(x \\in (A \\cup B) - C\\).\n\nThus, every element of \\((A - C) \\cup (B - C)\\) is in \\((A \\cup B) - C\\), proving \\((A - C) \\cup (B - C) \\subseteq (A \\cup B) - C\\).\n\nSince both inclusions are established, we conclude that \\((A \\cup B) - C = (A - C) \\cup (B - C)\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{(A \\cup B) - C = (A - C) \\cup (B - C)}\n\\]",
