id to each vertex is 2/3 of the height, which is (2/3)(3√3) = 2√3. So, each vertex is 2√3 units away from the centroid.\n\nSo, the triangular face has vertices 2√3 units away from the center (8, 2√3, 5). So, the coordinates of the vertices of the triangular face can be determined.\n\nBut maybe I don't need the exact coordinates. Instead, I can think about the movement of Prism 2 along the x-axis. As it slides towards Prism 1, the distance between the two prisms along the x-axis will decrease, and the distance along the z-axis is fixed at 10.\n\nWait, but the prisms are 3D objects, so their positions in 3D space will affect whether they collide. So, the collision will occur when the distance between the two prisms is zero, meaning they touch each other.\n\nSo, I need to model the positions of both prisms as functions of x, and find the x where the distance between them is zero.\n\nLet me denote the position of Prism 2 as (x, y, z). Since it's sliding along the x-axis, y and z are fixed. Wait, no, actually, the center is at (8, 2√3, 5), so as it slides, the x-coordinate changes, but y and z remain the same. So, the center is moving along the x-axis, but the y and z coordinates are fixed.\n\nWait, but the prism is a 3D object, so even if the center moves along the x-axis, the prism extends in the negative x-direction. So, the prism's position is determined by its center and its orientation.\n\nSince the prism is oriented along the negative x-direction, its length along the x-axis is 6 units, and its height along the z-axis is 10 units.\n\nSo, the prism extends from x = 8 - 6 = 2 to x = 8 + 6 = 14 along the x-axis, and from z = 5 - 10 = -5 to z = 5 + 10 = 15 along the z-axis.\n\nWait, but Prism 1 is fixed with its base in the xy-plane, so its z-coordinate ranges from 0 to 10. So, Prism 2 is moving towards Prism 1, which is at z=0 to z=10.\n\nWait, but Prism 2 is centered at (8, 2√3, 5), so its z-coordinate is 5, and it extends from z=5 - 10 = -5 to z=5 + 10 = 15. So, it