he statement is false, which is consistent with Ben lying. So, that's okay.\n\nSo, in this case, Ben's statement is false, which is consistent with him lying.\n\nSo, all statements hold in Case 1.\n\nIn Case 2, Ben's statement would be true, but Ben is supposed to be lying, so Case 2 is invalid.\n\nTherefore, the only consistent solution is Alice and Clara are truthful, Ben is lying.\n\nSo, to recap:\n\n- Alice: Truthful.\n\n- Ben: Lying.\n\n- Clara: Truthful.\n\nI think that's the only possible solution. Let me just make sure there are no other possibilities.\n\nWait, could there be a scenario where Clara is lying, but Alice is lying? Let me check.\n\nIf Clara is lying, then from her statement, Alice is lying. So, Alice is lying. From Alice's statement, if Ben is telling the truth, then Clara is lying. But if Alice is lying, then Ben could be either truthful or lying. Wait, no, from Alice's statement, if Ben is truthful, then Clara is lying. But if Alice is lying, then Ben could be either truthful or lying.\n\nWait, but from Ben's statement, we saw that if Ben is truthful, Alice is lying; if Ben is lying, Alice is truthful.\n\nSo, if Clara is lying, Alice is lying. So, if Alice is lying, Ben could be either truthful or lying.\n\nBut if Alice is lying, Ben could be truthful or lying. Let's see.\n\nIf Alice is lying, Ben could be truthful or lying.\n\nIf Ben is truthful, then from Ben's statement, Alice is lying, which is consistent.\n\nIf Ben is lying, then from Ben's statement, Alice is truthful, but Alice is actually lying. So, that would be a contradiction.\n\nTherefore, if Alice is lying, Ben must be truthful.\n\nSo, if Alice is lying, Ben is truthful.\n\nFrom Ben's statement, since Ben is truthful, Alice is lying, which is consistent.\n\nFrom Clara's statement, since Clara is lying, Alice and she are not both truthful or both lying. Since Alice is lying, Clara is lying, so they are both lying, which is consistent with Clara's statement.\n\nSo, in this case, Ali