      {
        "label": "deduction",
        "text": "Layer 1: 4\n\nLayer 2: 7, 10\n\nLayer 3: 13, 16, 19\n\nLayer 4: 22, 25, 28, 31\n\nI'll calculate the differences between consecutive numbers in each layer:\n\nLayer 1: 4\n\nLayer 2: 10 - 7 = 3, 13 - 10 = 3\n\nLayer 3: 16 - 13 = 3, 19 - 16 = 3\n\nLayer 4: 25 - 22 = 3, 28 - 25 = 3, 31 - 28 = 3\n\nSo, each layer has numbers that increase by 3 each time."
      },
      {
        "label": "initializing",
        "text": "Now, I'll determine the starting number of each layer."
      },
      {
        "label": "deduction",
        "text": "Layer 1 starts at 4.\n\nLayer 2 starts at 7, which is 4 + 3.\n\nLayer 3 starts at 13, which is 7 + 6.\n\nLayer 4 starts at 22, which is 13 + 9.\n\nI notice that the starting number increases by 3, 6, 9, etc., which are multiples of 3.\n\nThis suggests that the starting number of each layer is 3 more than the starting number of the previous layer.\n\nTherefore, the starting number of Layer n can be expressed as:\n\nStarting number = 4 + 3*(n - 1)"
      },
      {
        "label": "example-testing",
        "text": "For example:\n\nLayer 1: 4 + 3*(1 - 1) = 4\n\nLayer 2: 4 + 3*(2 - 1) = 7\n\nLayer 3: 4 + 3*(3 - 1) = 13\n\nLayer 4: 4 + 3*(4 - 1) = 22\n\nThis formula holds true for the given layers."
      },
      {
        "label": "deduction",
        "text": "Now, to find the numbers in Layer 6, I'll use the starting number formula:\n\nStarting number of Layer 6 = 4 + 3*(6 - 1) = 4 + 15 = 19\n\nSince each layer has 6 numbers and the numbers increase by 3 each time, the numbers in Layer 6 will be:\n\n19, 22, 25, 28, 31, 34"
      },
