 (4,1), down to (4,0), and then right to (4,-1).\n\nSo, the direction changes are: right, up, left, down, right, up, left, down, right, etc.\n\nSo, the direction cycles through right, up, left, down, repeating every four steps.\n\nBut let me check:\n\nFrom (1,1) to (1,2): right.\n\n(1,2) to (2,2): up.\n\n(2,2) to (2,1): left.\n\n(2,1) to (2,0): down.\n\n(2,0) to (3,0): right.\n\n(3,0) to (3,1): up.\n\n(3,1) to (3,2): left.\n\n(3,2) to (3,3): up.\n\n(3,3) to (4,3): right.\n\n(4,3) to (4,2): down.\n\n(4,2) to (4,1): left.\n\n(4,1) to (4,0): down.\n\n(4,0) to (4,-1): right.\n\nSo, the direction sequence is: right, up, left, down, right, up, left, down, right, etc.\n\nSo, every four steps, the direction cycles through right, up, left, down.\n\nBut wait, after (4,0), it goes to (4,-1), which is moving right again. So, the direction is right, which is the same as the first step.\n\nSo, the direction changes every four steps, cycling through right, up, left, down, and then repeating.\n\nSo, the spiral is moving in a square pattern, increasing the side length each time, and changing direction every four steps.\n\nSo, the next step after (4,-1) would be moving up again, right? Because the last direction was right, so the next direction would be up.\n\nWait, let me see:\n\nAfter (4,-1), moving right would take us to (5,-1). But let me check if that makes sense.\n\nWait, but let me think about the layers. Each layer is a square, and each time, the side length increases by 1.\n\nSo, the first layer is 1x1: (1,1).\n\nThe second layer is 2x2: (1,1) to (2,2).\n\nThe third layer is 3x3: (1,1) to (3,3).\n\nThe fourth layer is 4x4: (1,1) to (4,4).\n\nWait, but in the given sequence, the fourth layer is only up to (4,3). Hmm, maybe not.\n\nWait, perhaps each layer is a square, but the starting point is moving outward.\n\nWait, maybe it's better to think in terms of rings. The first ring is just (1,1). The second ring is from (1,2) to (2,2), (2,1), (2,0). The third ring is from (3,0) to (3,3). The fourth ring is from (4,3) to (4,4). Wait, but in the given sequence, after (4,3), it goes to (4,2), (4,1), (4,0), and then to (4,-1). So, perhaps the fourth ring is from (4,3) to (4,4), but in the given sequence, it's moving down to (4,-1). Hmm, that doesn't fit.\n\nWait, maybe the layers are not concentric squares but expanding in a different pattern.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the spiral is moving in a way that each layer adds a ring around the previous one, but the direction changes every four steps.\n\nWait, let me try to see the pattern of the coordinates.\n\nLooking at the given sequence:\n\n1. (1,1)\n2. (1,2)\n3. (2,2)\n4. (2,1)\n5. (2,0)\n6. (3,0)\n7. (3,1)\n8. (3,2)\n9. (3,3)\n10. (4,3)\n11. (4,2)\n12. (4,1)\n13. (4,0)\n14. (4,-1)\n\nSo, the first four steps are moving right, up, left, down.\n\nThen, the next four steps are moving right, up, left, down.\n\nWait, no, after (2,0), it goes to (3,0), which is right, then up, left, up, right, down, left, down, right, etc.\n\nWait, perhaps the direction changes every four steps, cycling through right, up, left, down, right, up, left, down, etc.\n\nSo, the direction sequence is: right, up, left, down, right, up, left, down, right, up, left, down, etc.\n\nSo, every four steps, the direction cycles.\n\nSo, the direction is determined by the step number modulo 4.\n\nIf we number the steps starting at 1:\n\nStep 1: right\n\nStep 2: up\n\nStep 3: left\n\nStep 4: down\n\nStep 5: right\n\nStep 6: up\n\nStep 7: left\n\nStep 8: down\n\nStep 9: right\n\nStep 10: up\n\nStep 11: left\n\nStep 12: down\n\nStep 13: right\n\nStep 14: up\n\nWait, but in the given sequence, step 14 is (4,-1), which is moving up from (4,0). So, step 14 is up.\n\nSo, the direction is right, up, left, down, right, up, left, down, right, up, left, down, right, up.\n\nSo, the direction is determined by (step number - 1) mod 4.\n\nIf we index steps starting at 0:\n\nStep 0: right\n\nStep 1: up\n\nStep 2: left\n\nStep 3: down\n\nStep 4: right\n\nStep 5: up\n\nStep 6: left\n\nStep 7: down\n\nStep 8: right\n\nStep 9: up\n\nStep 10: left\n\nStep 11: down\n\nStep 12: right\n\nStep 13: up\n\nSo, the direction is determined by (step number) mod 4, where 0 is right, 1 is up, 2 is left, 3 is down.\n\nSo, for each step, the direction is:\n\n- If step number mod 4 is 0: right\n\n- If step number mod 4 is 1: up\n\n- If step number mod 4 is 2: left\n\n- If step number mod 4 is 3: down\n\nSo, that's the direction rule.\n\nNow, to find the next five coordinate pairs, we need to continue this pattern.\n\nGiven the last step is (4,-1), which is step 14.\n\nSo, step 15: direction is (15 mod 4) = 15 - 3*4 = 15 -12=3, so direction is down.",
