cond layer is from (1,1) to (2,2), which is a 2x2 square. The third layer is from (1,1) to (3,3), which is a 3x3 square, and so on.\n\nWait, actually, the coordinates seem to be moving in a way that each layer adds a ring around the previous square. Each time, the side length increases by 1.\n\nBut looking at the given sequence, the first few layers are:\n\n- Layer 1: (1,1)\n- Layer 2: (1,2), (2,2), (2,1), (2,0)\n- Layer 3: (3,0), (3,1), (3,2), (3,3)\n- Layer 4: (4,3), (4,2), (4,1), (4,0)\n- Layer 5: (4,-1)\n\nWait, so each layer seems to be moving outward by one unit each time. So, the first layer is just (1,1). The second layer is from (1,2) to (2,2), (2,1), (2,0). The third layer is from (3,0) to (3,3). The fourth layer is from (4,3) to (4,-1).\n\nBut in the given sequence, after (4,3), it goes to (4,2), (4,1), (4,0), and then to (4,-1). So, it's moving down along the rightmost column.\n\nSo, perhaps each layer is a square, and the direction changes after completing each side.\n\nLet me try to map out the movement:\n\n1. Start at (1,1).\n2. Move right to (1,2).\n3. Move up to (2,2).\n4. Move left to (2,1).\n5. Move down to (2,0).\n6. Move right to (3,0).\n7. Move up to (3,1).\n8. Move left to (3,2).\n9. Move up to (3,3).\n10. Move right to (4,3).\n11. Move down to (4,2).\n12. Move left to (4,1).\n13. Move down to (4,0).\n14. Move right to (4,-1).\n\nWait, so after completing the top side of the square, it starts moving down. So, each time, after completing a side, it turns 90 degrees downward.\n\nBut let me think about the direction changes. Starting at (1,1), moving right, then up, then left, then down, then right, then up, then left, then down, etc.\n\nBut in the given sequence, after (2,0), it goes to (3,0), which is moving right. So, perhaps after completing the bottom side, it starts moving right again.\n\nWait, let me see:\n\nAfter (2,0), it goes to (3,0), which is moving right. Then up to (3,1), left to (3,2), up to (3,3), right to (4,3), down to (4,2), left to (4,1), down to (4,0), and then right to (4,-1).\n\nSo, the direction changes are: right, up, left, down, right, up, left, down, right, etc.\n\nSo, the direction cycles through right, up, left, down, repeating every four steps.\n\nBut let me check:\n\nFrom (1,1) to (1,2): right.\n\n(1,2) to (2,2): up.\n\n(2,2) to (2,1): left.\n\n(2,1) to (2,0): down.\n\n(2,0) to (3,0): right.\n\n(3,0) to (3,1): up.\n\n(3,1) to (3,2): left.\n\n(3,2) to (3,3): up.\n\n(3,3) to (4,3): right.\n\n(4,3) to (4,2): down.\n\n(4,2) to (4,1): left.\n\n(4,1) to (4,0): down.\n\n(4,0) to (4,-1): right.\n\nSo, the direction sequence is: right, up, left, down, right, up, left, down, right, etc.\n\nSo, every four steps, the direction cycles through right, up, left, down.\n\nBut wait, after (4,0), it goes to (4,-1), which is moving right again. So, the direction is right, which is the same as the first step.\n\nSo, the direction changes every four steps, cycling through right, up, left, down, and then repeating.\n\nSo, the spiral is moving in a square pattern, increasing the side length each time, and changing direction every four steps.\n\nSo, the next step after (4,-1) would be moving up again, right? Because the last direction was right, so the next direction would be up.\n\nWait, let me see:\n\nAfter (4,-1), moving right would take us to (5,-1). But let me check if that makes sense.\n\nWait, but let me think about the layers. Each layer is a square, and each time, the side length increases by 1.\n\nSo, the first layer is 1x1: (1,1).\n\nThe second layer is 2x2: (1,1) to (2,2).\n\nThe third layer is 3x3: (1,1) to (3,3).\n\nThe fourth layer is 4x4: (1,1) to (4,4).\n\nWait, but in the given sequence, the fourth layer is only up to (4,3). Hmm, maybe not.\n\nWait, perhaps each layer is a square, but the starting point is moving outward.\n\nWait, maybe it's better to think in terms of rings. The first ring is just (1,1). The second ring is from (1,2) to (2,2), (2,1), (2,0). The third ring is from (3