    "annotations": [
      {
        "label": "initializing",
        "text": "Okay, so I have this puzzle where there are three light switches in a room, and only one of them controls a light bulb in another room. The other room is completely sealed, so I can't see through it or check the bulb directly. I can flip the switches as many times as I want, but I can only go upstairs to check the bulb once. Hmm, interesting. I need to figure out a strategy to determine which switch controls the bulb with just one check."
      },
      {
        "label": "initializing",
        "text": "Let me start by thinking about the problem. There are three switches, let's call them Switch A, Switch B, and Switch C. Only one of these controls the bulb."
      },
      {
        "label": "adding-knowledge",
        "text": "The other two switches are probably just for flipping the light on or off without affecting the bulb."
      },
      {
        "label": "initializing",
        "text": "So, my goal is to figure out which one is the correct switch with just one trip upstairs."
      },
      {
        "label": "deduction",
        "text": "Since I can't see the room, I can't directly observe the bulb's state. But I can manipulate the switches and then check the bulb."
      },
      {
        "label": "initializing",
        "text": "Maybe I can use the process of elimination or some kind of timing to figure it out."
      },
      {
        "label": "uncertainty-estimation",
        "text": "Wait, but I can only go upstairs once. So, I can't do multiple experiments. That complicates things a bit."
      },
      {
        "label": "initializing",
        "text": "I need a method that allows me to deduce the correct switch without having to test each one individually."
      },
      {
        "label": "initializing",
        "text": "Let me think about the properties of the switches."
      },
      {
        "label": "adding-knowledge",
        "text": "Each switch can be in one of two states: on or off. If I flip a switch, it changes its state. So, if I flip a switch once, it turns on or off. If I flip it again, it goes back to the original state. So, if I flip a switch twice, it's like not flipping it at all."
      },
      {
        "label": "deduction",
        "text": "But since I can only go upstairs once, I can't flip a switch twice. So, I can only flip each switch once or not at all. That means I can only change the state of each switch once."
      },
      {
        "label": "uncertainty-estimation",
        "text": "Hmm, that might be a problem because if I flip a switch, it changes the state, but I don't know if that's the correct one or not."
      },
      {
        "label": "example-testing",
        "text": "Wait, maybe I can use the timing of the bulb's response. If I flip a switch, and then wait a certain amount of time, I can see if the bulb lights up."
      },
      {
        "label": "uncertainty-estimation",
        "text": "But since the room is sealed, I can't actually wait and see the bulb. So, that might not work."
      },
      {
        "label": "example-testing",
        "text": "Alternatively, maybe I can use the fact that the bulb will only light up if the switch is flipped."
      },
      {
        "label": "uncertainty-estimation",
        "text": "But since I can only flip each switch once, I can't be sure which one is the correct one."
      },
