Fiona, and George, three are true, one is false.\n\nBut if George is telling the truth, then exactly two people get promotions. So, let's see what that implies for the other statements.\n\nIf exactly two people get promotions, then let's see what Diana's statement says. Diana says either E or F, but not both. So, if exactly two people get promotions, and Diana is saying that either E or F is true, but not both, then Diana's statement would be true only if exactly one of E or F is true, which is consistent with exactly two people getting promotions because if two people are getting promotions, and Diana is saying that exactly one of E or F is true, then the other two promotions must be with Fiona and George, or Diana and George, or Diana and someone else.\n\nWait, hold on. If exactly two people get promotions, and Diana is saying that exactly one of E or F is true, then that would mean that the other two promotions must be with Fiona and George, or Diana and George, or Diana and someone else. Wait, but if exactly two people are getting promotions, and Diana is saying that exactly one of E or F is true, then the other two promotions must be with Fiona and George, or Diana and George, or Diana and someone else.\n\nWait, maybe I need to approach this differently.\n\nLet me consider the possible numbers of promotions. Since George is telling the truth, exactly two people get promotions. So, let's see what that implies for the other statements.\n\nIf exactly two people get promotions, then let's consider who could be getting the promotions.\n\nPossibility 1: Diana and George get promotions. So, D and G.\n\nThen, let's check the other statements.\n\nDiana's statement: Either E or F, but not both. If D and G are getting promotions, then E and F are not. So, Diana's statement is true because exactly one of E or F is true (which is zero in this case, but Diana is saying either E or F, but not both. So, since E and F are not getting promotions, Diana's statement is vacuously true because the condition is not met. Wait, no, Diana's statement is \"Either E or F will get the promotion, but not both.\" So, if neither E nor F get promotions, then Diana's statement is true because the condition is not met. So, Diana's statement is true.\n\nErik's statement: \"If I get the promotion, then George will not get it.\" So, if E is true, then G must be false. But in this case, E is false because only D and G are getting promotions. So, the implication is true regardless of G. So, since E is false, the implication is automatically true. So, Erik's statement is true.\n\nFiona's statement: \"Diana and I will both get promotions, or neither of us will.\" So, D and G are both getting promotions, so the first part is true. So, the statement is true.\n\nGeorge's statement: Exactly two people get promotions. So, in this case, D and G are getting promotions, which is exactly two. So, George's statement is true.\n\nSo, in this scenario, all four statements are true. But the problem states that exactly three are true and one is false. So, this can't be the case. Therefore, Diana and George cannot both be getting promotions because that would make all four statements true, which contradicts the given condition.\n\nPossibility 2: Diana and Fiona get promotions. So, D and F.\n\nThen, let's check the other statements.\n\nDiana's statement: Either E or F, but not both. Since F is getting a promotion, Diana's statement is claiming that E is not getting a promotion. So, Diana's statement is true because E is not getting a promotion, and F is getting one, so exactly one of E or F is getting a promotion. So, Diana's statement is true.\n\nErik's statement: \"If I get the promotion, then George will not get it.\" So, if E is true, then G must be false. In this case, E is false because only D and F are getting promotions. So, the implication is true regardless of G. So, since E is false, the implication is true. So, Erik's statement is true.\n\nFiona's statement: \"Diana and I will both get promotions, or neither of us will.\" So, D and F are both getting promotions, so the first part is true. So, the statement is true.\n\nGeorge's statement: Exactly two people get promotions. In this case, D and F are getting promotions, which is exactly two. So, George's statement is true.\n\nAgain, all four statements are true, which is not allowed. So, Diana and Fiona cannot both be getting promotions.\n\nPossibility 3: Diana and George are not both getting promotions. So, let's consider other possibilities.\n\nPossibility 4: Diana and someone else. Let's see.\n\nWait, maybe I need to consider different numbers of promotions. Since George is telling the truth, exactly two people get promotions. So, let's consider the possible pairs.\n\nWait, but in the previous cases, when I considered Diana and George, or Diana and Fiona, all led to all four statements being true, which is not allowed. So, maybe the only way for exactly two people to get promotions is if it's not Diana and George or Diana and Fiona.\n\nWait, but if exactly two people get promotions, and George is telling the truth, then the two promotions must be someone else. Let's see.\n\nPossibility 5: Fiona and someone else. Let's say Fiona and Erik.\n\nSo, F and E.\n\nThen, let's check the statements.\n\nDiana's statement: Either E or F, but not both. Since both E and F are getting promotions, Diana's statement is false because she's claiming that exactly one of E or F is getting a promotion, but both are. So, Diana's statement is false.\n\nErik's statement: \"If I get the promotion, then George will not get it.\" So, if E is true, then G must be false. In this case, E is true, and G is false (since only F and E are getting promotions). So, the implication holds because E is true and G is false. So, the implication is true. So, Erik's statement is true.\n\nFiona's statement: \"Diana and I will both get promotions, or neither of us will.\" So, D and F are both getting promotions, so the first part is true. So, the statement is true.\n\nGeorge's statement: Exactly two people get promotions. In this case, F and E are getting promotions, which is exactly two. So, George's statement is true.\n\nSo, in this scenario, Diana's statement is false, while the others are true. So, exactly one statement is false, which fits the condition. So, this seems to be a possible scenario.\n\nWait, let me double-check.\n\nIf Fiona and Erik are getting promotions, then:\n\n- Diana's statement: \"Either E or F, but not both.\" Since both E and F are getting promotions, Diana's statement is false.\n\n- Erik's statement: \"If I get the promotion, then George will not get it.\" Since E is true and G is false, the implication holds, so it's true.\n\n- Fiona's statement: \"Diana and I will both get promotions, or neither of us will.\" Since both D and F are getting promotions, the first part is true, so the statement is true.\n\n- George's statement: Exactly two people get promotions. Since F and E are getting promotions, which is exactly two, George's statement is true.\n\nSo, in this case, only Diana's statement is false, and the rest are true. That fits the condition of exactly three true and one false.\n\nBut wait, let me check if this is the only possibility.\n\nPossibility 6: Fiona and George. So, F and G.\n\nThen, let's check the statements.\n\nDiana's statement: \"Either E or F, but not both.\" Since F is getting a promotion, Diana's statement is claiming that E is not getting a promotion. So, Diana's statement is true because E is not getting a promotion, and F is getting one, so exactly one of E or F is getting a promotion. So, Diana's statement is true.\n\nErik's statement: \"If I get the promotion, then George will not get it.\" So, if E is true, then G must be false. In this case, E is false because only F and G are getting promotions. So, the implication is true regardless of G. So, since E is false, the implication is true. So, Erik's statement is true.\n\nFiona's s