ld develop these skills more effectively.\n\nAnother angle is the role of the structured activity. The board games are strategic, which might help with planning and logical thinking, which are important for arithmetic. But the control group didn't have structured activities, so maybe the lack of structure was a hindrance.\n\nWait, but the children who played with parents showed improvement in specific areas. So, perhaps the interaction with the game itself, especially when parents are involved, is more effective for certain skills than structured activities alone.\n\nI'm also thinking about the timing. The board game group spent one hour less on TV and video games. Maybe that time was used for other activities, like socializing or family time, which could have contributed to better math performance.\n\nSo, putting it all together, it seems like the interaction between the board game and the parents' involvement is a key factor. The parents' involvement might enhance the learning experience, making the board game more effective for certain math skills. The lack of structured activity in the control group might have hindered the development of arithmetic skills, but the strategic nature of the board game could compensate for that.\n\nAs for the experimental design, maybe a quasi-experimental design would be better. Since there's no random assignment, it's a bit tricky. Maybe a pretest-posttest design with a control group and a group that received parental involvement. Alternatively, a randomized controlled trial where parents are randomly assigned to either play the game or not, but that might not be feasible because parents can't be randomly assigned.\n\nAlternatively, a natural experiment approach, where the children who played with parents are compared to those who didn't, but the control group is the one without structured activities. That could help isolate the effect of playing with parents versus structured activities.\n\nI think the key is to look at the interaction between the board game and the parents' involvement. The parents' involvement might be the causal factor, as they can provide additional support and guidance, which enhances the learning experience and effectiveness of the board game.\n</think>\n\nThe improved math performance in the children who played strategic board games with parents' involvement can be attributed to the interaction between the board game and the parents' involvement. Parents' involvement likely enhances the learning experience by providing additional support and guidance, which can make the board game more effective for certain math skills, such as geometry and spatial reasoning. This interaction may compensate for the lack of structured activities in the control group, allowing the strategic nature of the board game to develop arithmetic skills. \n\nFor a better causal design, a natural experiment approach would be ideal. This involves comparing the children who played with parents to those who didn't, focusing on the board game's interaction with parents. This design can isolate the effect of parental involvement on math performance, providing a clearer causal pathway.",
