        "text": "So, I need to find P(Positive), which is the total probability of testing positive. This can happen in two ways: either the person has the disease and tests positive, or they don't have the disease but test positive.\n\nSo, P(Positive) = P(Positive | D) * P(D) + P(Positive | ~D) * P(~D)\n\nPlugging in the numbers:\n\nP(Positive) = (0.99 * 0.0001) + (0.01 * 0.9999)\n\nLet me compute each part:\n\n0.99 * 0.0001 = 0.000099\n\n0.01 * 0.9999 = 0.009999\n\nAdding them together: 0.000099 + 0.009999 = 0.010098\n\nSo, P(Positive) = 0.010098\n\nNow, going back to Bayes' theorem:\n\nP(D | Positive) = (0.99 * 0.0001) / 0.010098\n\nWe already calculated the numerator as 0.000099.\n\nSo, P(D | Positive) = 0.000099 / 0.010098\n\nLet me compute that division.\n\nFirst, let's write it as 0.000099 ÷ 0.010098.\n\nI can simplify this by multiplying numerator and denominator by 1,000,000 to eliminate decimals:\n\n99 ÷ 10098\n\nHmm, 99 divided by 10098.\n\nLet me compute that.\n\n10098 ÷ 99 is approximately 102. So, 99 ÷ 10098 is approximately 1/102, which is about 0.0098039.\n\nWait, let me do the division properly.\n\n10098 ÷ 99:\n\n99 goes into 100 once (99), remainder 1.\n\nBring down the 9: 19. 99 goes into 19 zero times. So, 0.\n\nBring down the 8: 198. 99 goes into 198 exactly 2 times.\n\nSo, 10098 ÷ 99 = 102.\n\nTherefore, 99 ÷ 10098 = 1/102 ≈ 0.0098039.\n\nSo, approximately 0.0098039, which is about 0.98039%."
